1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Hey, you're still there. huh,

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Remember me

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
How you doing. This is podcast number 352..

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast. This is Clark Greene. It's been a busy summer on many fronts and I'm going to devote the entirety of this podcast to catching up,

6
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Because it's not summer anymore, is it?

7
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:10,000
I want to talk through a few issues and events and then we'll talk about what's been going on at scoutmastercgcom and where we go from here.

8
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
I had a great trip to Kondersteg International Scout Center in Switzerland.

9
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
The troop had a great week at summer camp and they've laid the foundations for a great year of activities for our scouts.

10
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:34,000
The BSA began a listening campaign around the idea of quote serving the whole family, unquote, which is basically allowing girls to register in all of our age divisions.

11
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:42,000
There was a national jamboree where somebody spoke and there was an issue,

12
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:52,000
And I'm going to touch very briefly on all of those things, but first we took 22 people to Kondersteg International Scout Center in the village of Kondersteg, Switzerland, this summer.

13
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:01,000
We had a lovely time. If you have been a long time listener, you know that we do this as a high adventure trip every third summer

14
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
And I've talked at length about those trips in the past and you can find stuff online.

15
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:15,000
I did a set of videos about them on our last trip- not this year's trip, but on our last trip. that kind of give you an idea of what we do.

16
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
It's just a great activity. It's a great place to go. You get to meet scouts from all over the world.

17
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,000
You get to be in this world-class area of Switzerland. It's an amazing, amazing trip.

18
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
It was great fun. I did not get down to summer camp with the troop this year.

19
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:42,000
If you're following my own personal scouting career, I did retire as our scout master three years ago.

20
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:58,000
I came back to fill in over the 2016 and 2017 school year and I was happily able to retire a second time without quite so much fanfare,

21
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,000
because the troop has another new scout master now and that's going extraordinarily well.

22
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:13,000
So I'm not quite as involved in the day-to-day, week-to-week operations of the troop and the venture crew as I once was,

23
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,000
and I'm happy to see other people stepping up and taking it over and making it work.

24
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Now, whether you know about it or not, the BSA started this kind of listening campaign over the summer

25
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
where they're talking about the issue of serving the whole family,

26
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:39,000
And I know that very specifically they're talking about whether or not to open our membership across all of our age divisions to girls,

27
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:44,000
And I'm very happy to hear at least we're discussing this. so let me weigh in on a few things.

28
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:52,000
When I go visit Condorsteg, we're there with- oh, somewhere between three and four hundred scouts from all over Europe

29
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
and other parts of the world for that week.

30
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
The norm is in the rest of the world, scouting organizations are co-eds,

31
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:03,000
So they have girls and boys in scout troops together, believe it or not,

32
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,000
And it works. It works really well. It's not all that mysterious or difficult. It's great.

33
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
I've had the honor of speaking with scouts from all over the world.

34
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
You've heard some of them interviewed on the podcast and I can tell you that, in my opinion,

35
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
this step by the BSA is not only going to make scouting better here in the United States.

36
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
it's going to be much easier and much better than anybody can really imagine.

37
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:30,000
It's going to be great.

38
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
I'm going to talk at a little more length about four things about this,

39
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,000
because you run into four objections or issues about this whenever it comes up.

40
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:46,000
First, I've heard some talk about the social and emotional development differences in boys and girls.

41
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,000
I believe that's a thing I understand, that. I think that's demonstrable.

42
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,000
But where I think we're getting it wrong is I've heard some people say

43
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:59,000
if we're going to offer membership to girls across all of our programs,

44
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:04,000
we're going to have to take this developmental difference into account

45
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
and guarantee a level playing field for the boys,

46
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,000
who some believe generally develop emotionally and socially slower than girls.

47
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
In short, I've heard the assertion that girls would just take all of the leadership positions

48
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
and leave the boys out in the cold.

49
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
I don't think there's a whole lot of merit to the argument

50
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:26,000
because right now we're serving boys of differing levels of ability and development.

51
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,000
We don't presently allocate opportunity according to those differences. right

52
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
To do so with girls doesn't make any sense to me.

53
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
It would be inherently sexist. It would fail miserably.

54
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
I just think it's a bad way to move forward

55
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
When this happens. a scout is a scout.

56
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
There you go Full stop

57
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Now. second, there will be arguments against this based on the idea

58
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,000
that boys have to have some exclusive male atmosphere

59
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
away from girls and women to develop as men.

60
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
This was the same argument I heard when women were first permitted

61
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
to become scoutmasters 30 years ago.

62
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
It wasn't true then, It's not true now.

63
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
If boys can only develop into the right kind of men

64
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
because they are allotted some time in all male bastions,

65
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
away from feminine influences,

66
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
then I think we have to seriously question

67
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
the kind of men we want them to become

68
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
and the idea of manhood or masculinity that advances.

69
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Third, there's going to be an argument that while the rest of the world

70
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,000
is scouting and co-ed, the transition that other programs had

71
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
from single sex to co-ed was fraught with huge losses in membership,

72
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
a lot of disruption in the scouting organizations.

73
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Talking with youth-facing volunteers like you and I,

74
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,000
scouters who are in troops and packs and crews in the rest of the world

75
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
who were around for these transitions,

76
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:54,000
I think maybe that's partially true, and only partially.

77
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
But let's be real about the BSA

78
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:01,000
because nationally we haven't seen a positive growth year in two decades

79
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
so far as I know.

80
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,000
It's become increasingly difficult to find national membership numbers.

81
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,000
The BSA has always been a little recalcitrant

82
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,000
about publishing those numbers-

83
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:17,000
but that we haven't seen a positive growth year in a couple of decades nationally

84
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
I know there's been local areas of growth where membership grows,

85
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
but I'm talking on a national basis.

86
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
My best guess is two decades because you know what,

87
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:30,000
If we had a year where our national membership grew

88
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,000
in the past couple of decades, we would certainly hear about it.

89
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Now there's a lot of reasons why this is happening.

90
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
There's not a single reason

91
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
In the context of what we're talking about today.

92
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
we're talking about being a co-ed organization,

93
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:48,000
and is the fact that we're presently a single-sex organization

94
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
and most of our programs a factor in our limited membership growth

95
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
or our membership decline?

96
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
I think it is. I mean, it only makes sense, right,

97
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,000
If you make the program available to more children,

98
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
you can have more members, at least potentially.

99
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
But when it comes down to it, this decision should not solely be a question

100
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
of metrics and memberships

101
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,000
and whether or not it's going to make some people uncomfortable.

102
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:14,000
It should be a question of what's the right thing to do.

103
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:17,000
And let's remember: organizations are just organizations.

104
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
They are not scouting.

105
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
Scouting started without them.

106
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
Scouting will move on without them, if need be.

107
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:28,000
Organizations are great advanceers and containers of scouting,

108
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
great enablers of scouting,

109
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
provide opportunities for young people to be scouts,

110
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
But scouting is.

111
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,000
But organizational disruption is not going to kill scouting,

112
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
Never has, never will.

113
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
Fourth and finally, there's going to be an argument

114
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
that other societies and cultures are simply better suited to this change,

115
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,000
while ours is not.

116
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,000
Now, if you think about this,

117
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
you're really not aware of what's going on,

118
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,000
because I have welcomed the incredibly positive

119
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
and affirmative changes

120
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
that our society and culture has made

121
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
over the past decade or more

122
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,000
when it comes to gender, when it comes to sexual orientation.

123
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Those changes are going to keep going

124
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:16,000
and our young people are way, way past us,

125
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,000
especially past our organization, on these issues,

126
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:25,000
And it's been way too long since we have tried to catch up.

127
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
Certainly, there's going to be a lot of discussion

128
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:31,000
and there's going to be some controversy as this happens,

129
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
But as far as I'm concerned, who ray for co-ed scouting?

130
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
It can't happen soon enough.

131
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
It should be implemented at the organizational level

132
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
as simply and as quickly as possible,

133
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
because it's going to mean a lot.

134
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
It's going to make the program better for our boys

135
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
and it's going to mean a lot for our girls.

136
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,000
I also think it's worth considering for a moment

137
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
the kind of severe political and cultural unrest

138
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
that we've seen this year in the division

139
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
that we've experienced as a country,

140
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:05,000
because it was highlighted in the world of scouting

141
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
during the national jamboree with the president's speech.

142
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
I'm not going to opine about the content of the speech,

143
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
the reactions to it or anything like that.

144
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Let's just use that as a touch point

145
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
to talk about the broader issue,

146
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
because when this kind of thing happens,

147
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
when we're experiencing this kind of division and unrest,

148
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:26,000
young people we serve are going to be looking for answers

149
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
and trying to sort things out

150
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
and, no doubt, expressing their personal opinions

151
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
on recent events.

152
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
So what is a scouter's role

153
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
dealing with these kinds of issues?

154
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
Well, it's not to advance our personal political,

155
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
religious or social position,

156
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
but to help the young people we serve

157
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
sort these things out for themselves.

158
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
We're fortunate to have some very powerful tools

159
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
to do just that,

160
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
and those tools are the scout oath and law.

161
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
If we're wise,

162
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
rather than expressing our own personal political opinions

163
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
or social opinions or religious opinions,

164
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:05,000
we will use the scout oath and law wisely

165
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
to help young people sort out on their own

166
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
what they are thinking and feeling about things.

167
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
If we wield these tools deftly and skillfully,

168
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
we can accomplish this without espousing

169
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,000
a particular political, religious or cultural position.

170
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,000
If and when you end up in a discussion

171
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
with the youth that you serve

172
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
about issues like this,

173
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
honor the commitment that you've made as a scouter

174
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
by focusing on using the oath and law

175
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
to define these events,

176
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
rather than trying to promulgate your own personal point of view,

177
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
your own politics,

178
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
your own take on a social issue.

179
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
So here are three things to remember to make that work.

180
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Don't make statements as much as ask questions.

181
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:53,000
If you're confronted with discussions of things like this,

182
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
don't make statements about them.

183
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
Ask questions of the young people.

184
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
find out how they understand them.

185
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,000
The second thing to do is to make sure you listen

186
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
to those answers.

187
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
I am one of those people who are guilty of forming

188
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
what I am going to say

189
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
in answer to a question

190
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
as the question is being asked,

191
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
and I kind of forget to listen sometimes.

192
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
And third, lay aside your own opinions and ideas,

193
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
become a neutral observer

194
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
and explore what your scouts are thinking and feeling.

195
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
Doing those three things focuses on what we're charged with

196
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
and what we promise to do as scouts,

197
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,000
and that is enable our scouts to develop character

198
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
through the scout oath and law,

199
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,000
and not to promote a particular point of view

200
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
or a specific way of seeing the world,

201
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
Because when we have political unrest,

202
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
when we have divisiveness,

203
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,000
we're not only going to be calmed by people of goodwill

204
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,000
who strive for ideals that we can all hold in common:

205
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
The ideals of the scout. oath and law

206
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
have this nearly universal appeal

207
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
that overcomes divisions,

208
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
and if we apply them wisely,

209
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
we can become a great uniting force

210
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
in times when we're most needed.

211
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Okay, so that's what's been going on

212
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
in the wider world of things.

213
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
Now give me a minute or two to talk about

214
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
what's happening as far as I'm concerned

215
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
and what's going on at scoutmastercgcom.

216
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
First of all, I've been touched and very thankful

217
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
for all of those of you gotten in touch with me

218
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
over the summer to ask,

219
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:27,000
basically, are you okay?

220
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
What's going on

221
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
Well? first, everything's good,

222
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Everything's great.

223
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
In June I decided I was going to take a break.

224
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,000
We were getting ready to head to Kondersteg,

225
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
and then I found excuses,

226
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
as the summer went by, to keep extending the break.

227
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and now here we are in September.

228
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
Well, forgive me for that,

229
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
but it's been very refreshing

230
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,000
and I'm ready to get back to podcasting on a regular basis.

231
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
I don't know at this point

232
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
if it will be like I'm going back

233
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
to doing a weekly podcast,

234
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
and here's why

235
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
I have a couple of non-scout projects

236
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
I've been pursuing over the past year

237
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
that are demanding more of my attention,

238
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
and I'm going to continue working on them

239
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:11,000
and at some point maybe I'll tell you more about these,

240
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
but right now let me just leave that there.

241
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:17,000
Second, this is podcast number 352

242
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:20,000
and frankly, I'm running out of new things

243
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
to say that I believe are useful to you

244
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
every single week.

245
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,000
I mean, if you look at the whole back catalog of podcasts,

